To resolve the "Unsupported field value: undefined" error in Firestore, we need to ensure that no `undefined` values are passed to the `set` or `update` methods. Firestore does not support `undefined`; optional fields should be omitted or set to `null`.

The most robust fix is to sanitize the data object right before it is saved in `services/authService.ts`, stripping out any keys with `undefined` values.

Here are the changes:

# Updated files:

## services/authService.ts

Added a `removeUndefined` helper function and applied it inside `saveUserData` to sanitize the payload before sending it to Firestore.

```typescript
import { auth, db } from './firebase';
import type { UserDetails } from '../types';
import { EducationalLevel } from '../types';
import { initializeUsage } from '../lib/usageManager';
import firebase from 'firebase/compat/app';

// Helper to remove undefined fields recursively
const removeUndefined = (obj: any): any => {
    if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') {
        return obj;
    }

    if (Array.isArray(obj)) {
        return obj.map(removeUndefined);
    }

    // Preserve Date objects
    if (obj instanceof Date) return obj;

    // Preserve Firestore FieldValues (sentinels) if any are passed (rare in this service but good for safety)
    // Checking for constructor name is a heuristic
    if (obj.constructor && obj.constructor.name === 'FieldValue') return obj;

    const newObj: any = {};
    Object.keys(obj).forEach((key) => {
        const value = obj[key];
        if (value !== undefined) {
            newObj[key] = removeUndefined(value);
        }
    });
    return newObj;
};

